{
  "id": "possessives-a1",
  "level": "a1",
  "area": "First words, first sentences",
  "title": "my, your, his, her and 's",
  "tagline": "Who does it belong to? my, your, his, her, its, our, their, and the little apostrophe s.",
  "cefr": "A1",
  "exam": [
    "Reading Part 5 — open cloze (grammar gaps)",
    "Listening Part 1 — matching people and their things"
  ],
  "why": "You need possessives every time you talk about your family, your friends and your things: <i>my sister, her bike, Mateo's book</i>. Spanish <i>su</i> can mean his, her, its or their, so this is one of the easiest places for a Spanish speaker to make a small, confusing mistake.",
  "hook": {
    "text": "At Sofia's birthday party, the presents are mixed up! \"Is this <b>your</b> present, Liam?\" asks Nadia. \"No, that's <b>Mateo's</b>. <b>His</b> present is the blue one.\" \"<b>Whose</b> is this big one?\" \"That's <b>Sofia's</b> — it's <b>her</b> present!\"",
    "ask": "Find a possessive adjective (like his or her), a word with an apostrophe s, and the question word that asks about owners."
  },
