  "can_do": [
    "I can say who things belong to with <i>my, your, his, her, its, our</i> and <i>their</i>.",
    "I can use <i>'s</i> to show who owns something, like <i>Sofia's bag</i> or <i>my parents' car</i>.",
    "I can ask <i>Whose is this?</i> and use <i>me, him, her, us</i> and <i>them</i> after a verb or preposition."
  ],
  "summary": [
    "Possessive adjectives go before the noun: <i>my, your, his, her, its, our, their</i> + noun.",
    "One owner: name + <b>'s</b> (<i>Sofia's bag</i>). Plural owner ending in -s: just add <b>'</b> (<i>my parents' car</i>). Plural not ending in -s: add <b>'s</b> (<i>children's toys</i>).",
    "<b>His</b> is for a boy or man, <b>her</b> for a girl or woman, <b>its</b> for a thing or animal.",
    "<b>Whose…?</b> asks who owns something — don't confuse it with <i>Who…?</i>.",
    "After a verb or preposition, use the object pronoun: <i>me, you, him, her, it, us, them</i>."
  ]
}
